{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:35:33.800681Z","id":"06516bb14faf65280fd7a926f1fd09905779b322e95e0c5dcd2b90bf90ed4dcd","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/postgres is the overlay docs/kubernetes.md tells operators to apply for production, and it sets CLAX_POSTGRES_TLS to \"off\" (main.rs's default too). Every tenant's SQLite pages, artifact content, app data and the fleet's leases then cross the network in plaintext, to a managed database that is usually outside the cluster. postgres-dev is the overlay where an in-cluster plaintext database is expected.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":68},"end":{"line":71},"content_hash":"64071d067a3c015e63707ae856f9d15ab811290039db9d94ccbe0cd140d4c562"},"suggested_fix":"Set CLAX_POSTGRES_TLS to \"on\" in deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml (with the sslmode fix in pg.rs so on means required) and turn it off in deploy/k8s/postgres-dev with a patch; say in docs/kubernetes.md that off is for in-cluster databases only.","summary":"The production PostgreSQL overlay connects to the database without TLS by default","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:35:33.800949Z","id":"b735b950ce14f9374b8d2171fefa303f3e8808c61bf40053595991e51f362e5a","body":{"detail":"Both workloads (here and deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:36) use ghcr.io/empathic/clax-fleet:latest, so a pod restart can pull a different binary than its peers run (and than was reviewed); deploy/Dockerfile:11, 18 and 26 pull node, rust and debian by tag, which their publishers can move. A fleet's pods also must agree on the fleet schema and forwarding headers, which mixed versions break.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":45},"end":{"line":45},"content_hash":"5be5484c159e8eb07ff75e3da8843103827220a93f34e31baffd33d421367846"},"suggested_fix":"Ship the manifests with a versioned tag (set by kustomize images in the release) and pin the Dockerfile's base images by digest (FROM debian:bookworm-slim@sha256:...).","summary":"The manifests run a mutable :latest image, and the Dockerfile builds from base images by tag","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:36:07.214659Z","id":"ce8b2ed2c0dbebbbe20495fd3a8ba641016a8955e5a9d3ee92f97a85c6973111","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/postgres/hpa.yaml scales the Deployment from 2 to 10, and the catalog is changed by editing base/fleet.toml and re-applying the overlay (docs/kubernetes.md:58-85). The Kubernetes docs say that with an HPA spec.replicas should be removed from the manifest, because 'any time a change to that object is applied ... this will instruct Kubernetes to scale the current number of Pods to the value of the spec.replicas key' (https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/horizontal-pod-autoscale/#migrating-deployments-and-statefulsets-to-horizontal-autoscaling). Here a scale-down is costly: every pod removed hands its instances over and cuts their streams (hpa.yaml:23-29 slows the HPA's own scale-down for that reason), and an apply bypasses that slowing.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":6},"end":{"line":6},"content_hash":"7013cc511070b80819e7b756d64664c0d744faeac5bbeb5145afff1dbee6f7b0"},"suggested_fix":"Drop `replicas` from deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml (the HPA's minReplicas sets the floor), and note in docs/kubernetes.md that a fork without the HPA must add it back.","summary":"The Deployment sets replicas: 2 beside its HorizontalPodAutoscaler, so each `kubectl apply -k` resets a scaled-out fleet to 2 pods and moves instances","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:36:07.214844Z","id":"593ce451cf15db58c3e6188eac994e7e29b4b83767e051a4ecd8d3eb45fbe77e","body":{"detail":"On PostgreSQL an instance may hold SQLite's 64 MiB page cache (crates/clax-pagevfs/src/lib.rs:112), a 32 MiB chunk cache (crates/clax-fleet/src/pages.rs:28-29), up to 64 open app-data stores each on its own page-VFS connection (crates/clax-fleet/src/app_data.rs:21-22) and transactions of up to 64 MiB buffered (clax-pagevfs lib.rs:115). Rendezvous hashing spreads instances by count, and the HPA (hpa.yaml) scales on CPU only, so 20 or so busy instances per pod can pass 2 Gi. An OOM-killed pod's instances move to the others, which raises their memory in turn. docs/kubernetes.md gives no sizing guidance per instance for either backend.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":92},"end":{"line":97},"content_hash":"a421a00bd3134e04df35900625bb8582f4dbc5d6a958beeeef4d943729343a4e"},"suggested_fix":"Document a per-instance memory figure and size requests and limits from instances per pod; add memory to the HPA's metrics; and consider bounding the caches per pod (a shared budget) rather than per instance.","summary":"The 2 Gi memory limit is not tied to how many instances a pod runs, and nothing caps instances per pod, so a fleet with many instances OOM-kills its pods in turn","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:36:07.214864Z","id":"b45eee462c68c3928315861cedbbdc35329ce556137f7b7ba94f58040a989b44","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/postgres is the overlay for a real database (postgres-dev/ is the one for trying it out), yet it sets CLAX_POSTGRES_TLS=off, the binary's default too (crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:111-113), so the password and every instance's pages cross the network in clear unless the operator finds this variable. Operators of a managed database put `?sslmode=require` in the connection string; here TLS is a separate variable, and with it off the pool connects with postgres::NoTls (crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs:58-63). docs/kubernetes.md:130-132 also leaves mounting CLAX_POSTGRES_CA to the reader, with no volume in the manifests while the root filesystem is read-only.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":68},"end":{"line":71},"content_hash":"64071d067a3c015e63707ae856f9d15ab811290039db9d94ccbe0cd140d4c562"},"suggested_fix":"Default the postgres overlay to CLAX_POSTGRES_TLS=on (keep off in postgres-dev), honour sslmode in the URL (require/verify-full turns TLS on, disable turns it off) or refuse a URL whose sslmode contradicts CLAX_POSTGRES_TLS, and add a commented Secret volume for the CA at a fixed path.","summary":"The production PostgreSQL overlay connects to the database without TLS, and the URL's standard sslmode is not the switch","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:36:07.215018Z","id":"c893b1fea246298be110fc594aeb0d4d5d1e08ae16f743355b1f7f0f948b7f46","body":{"detail":"During a rollout (maxSurge 1, maxUnavailable 0) instances move to the new pods as they settle (coord::SETTLE, 30 s) and each one is migrated by the new binary when it starts there. If the new release misbehaves and is rolled back, or a new pod dies and its instances return to old pods, the old binary meets a newer schema: clax-store refuses it (CoreError::SchemaNewer, crates/clax-store/src/lib.rs:810-811) and the instance stays down, and pg::migrate likewise refuses a newer fleet schema (crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs:328-331), so old pods cannot even start. docs/kubernetes.md:162-165 mentions only taking a dump first. On SQLite every shard restarts in turn with no handover, so each shard's instances are down for a restart; the docs do not say so either.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":7},"end":{"line":11},"content_hash":"da548f9855c3d03cf2e599d478dc6725bda1e4f65f17a3f4c4a2984020db03a7"},"suggested_fix":"Add an Upgrading section to docs/kubernetes.md: take the dump, roll out, and how to roll back (restore the dump into a fleet scaled to zero) once instances migrated; state that SQLite upgrades take each shard's instances down for its restart; and say in release notes when a release migrates.","summary":"No documented upgrade procedure, and a rolling upgrade that migrates instances cannot be rolled back","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:15.292951Z","id":"c29022f9d492ac725fef7bab53d522e2eec1fee447c6a6acf1d39e465760e730","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml:70-71 sets CLAX_POSTGRES_TLS value \"off\"; crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:112 defaults it to off too; docs/kubernetes.md:120-125 presents deploy/k8s/postgres as the non-dev overlay and postgres-dev as 'not for production'. With Tls::Off pg.rs:61 connects with postgres::NoTls.","kind":"comment","references":"06516bb14faf65280fd7a926f1fd09905779b322e95e0c5dcd2b90bf90ed4dcd","summary":"confirmed: the production postgres overlay sets CLAX_POSTGRES_TLS to off","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:15.295691Z","id":"7980a9b44def2a488e8ed3e7abb61ef35dffe5687872da7728f5f272dc06e00f","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml:45 and deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:36 use ghcr.io/empathic/clax-fleet:latest, and no kustomization sets images; deploy/Dockerfile:11, 18, 26 are FROM node:22.22.0-bookworm-slim, rust:1.94.0-bookworm, debian:bookworm-slim with no digest. docs/kubernetes.md:52 leaves the tag to an operator overlay.","kind":"comment","references":"b735b950ce14f9374b8d2171fefa303f3e8808c61bf40053595991e51f362e5a","summary":"confirmed: both workloads use :latest and the Dockerfile pulls base images by tag","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:04.886312Z","id":"9e8743a1df70802538b86995642b580ecb7f9afaf9a45c62e730ec341a0f7dfc","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml:6 sets replicas: 2; deploy/k8s/postgres/hpa.yaml targets Deployment clax with minReplicas 2, maxReplicas 10 and a slowed scaleDown (one pod per 120 s after a 300 s window). kubectl apply sets every field in the configuration whose value differs from the live object (https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/manage-kubernetes-objects/declarative-config/#how-apply-calculates-differences-and-merges-changes), and the HPA page says to remove spec.replicas for that reason (https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/horizontal-pod-autoscale/#migrating-deployments-and-statefulsets-to-horizontal-autoscaling). docs/kubernetes.md:60-85 changes the catalog by editing base/fleet.toml and re-applying.","kind":"comment","references":"ce8b2ed2c0dbebbbe20495fd3a8ba641016a8955e5a9d3ee92f97a85c6973111","summary":"confirmed: replicas: 2 beside the HPA resets a scaled-out Deployment on each apply","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:04.888040Z","id":"bc68cdb2e6944a4581dbeab7956dbdad0925fb7f394ad887158006fc0748b6b3","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml:92-97: requests 512Mi, limits.memory 2Gi. crates/clax-pagevfs/src/lib.rs:112 CACHE_BYTES = 64 MiB per connection and :116 MAX_TRANSACTION_BYTES = 64 MiB; crates/clax-fleet/src/pages.rs:28-29 CACHE_CHUNKS 8192 (32 MiB) per instance; crates/clax-fleet/src/app_data.rs:21-22 OPEN_STORES = 64 per instance. deploy/k8s/postgres/hpa.yaml has only a cpu metric. grep -n -i 'memory|sizing' docs/kubernetes.md finds only line 225 (an in-memory coordinator).","kind":"comment","references":"593ce451cf15db58c3e6188eac994e7e29b4b83767e051a4ecd8d3eb45fbe77e","summary":"confirmed: per-instance caches are unbounded per pod against a fixed 2 Gi limit, with no sizing guidance","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:04.888374Z","id":"d725c665a59792f9b88ee73809905b82c0e4d49bf5824837f3bbf356c6b00156","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml:68-71 sets CLAX_POSTGRES_TLS \"off\"; crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:111-113 defaults it to off. crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs:57-63 connects with postgres::NoTls whenever tls is None, so a URL with sslmode=require gets no TLS connector (the connection fails instead of using TLS). grep -rn 'sslmode|ssl_mode|SslMode' crates/clax-fleet/src finds only .qual records. No volume for a CA exists in the deployment's volumes (deployment.yaml data, catalog, tmp only) while readOnlyRootFilesystem is true.","kind":"comment","references":"b45eee462c68c3928315861cedbbdc35329ce556137f7b7ba94f58040a989b44","summary":"confirmed: the production overlay sets CLAX_POSTGRES_TLS=off, and sslmode in the URL does not turn TLS on","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:21.161275Z","id":"2d60bfbb46799da36866be11b2dfc6b9d9323539ffb3b306e5451d20fe4eeddf","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-store/src/lib.rs:810-811 documents refusing a database whose schema is newer (CoreError::SchemaNewer); crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs:328-331 returns an error when clax_fleet_schema's version exceeds SCHEMA_VERSION. deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml:7-11 rolls with maxSurge 1, maxUnavailable 0. grep -n -i 'upgrad|rollout|roll back' docs/kubernetes.md finds only line 165 (dump before upgrading) and 211 (migration on first open).","kind":"comment","references":"c893b1fea246298be110fc594aeb0d4d5d1e08ae16f743355b1f7f0f948b7f46","summary":"confirmed: no upgrade or rollback procedure, and an older binary refuses a migrated schema","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
